king is  included  material is fed through forced air dry  towers  and then rolled up on what we call a  take-up  in addition to the features  i just mentioned on the other slide  other features can be added depending on  application  for example for poor tensile strength  material  ultra low tension options are available  which reduce inertial force  on the membrane  humidity control  enclosures can be included around  dispensing and take a module  and for many applications  where moisture is critical a moisture  sensor can be added inline  a newer trend in inline manufacturing is  plc systems  in order for proper control monitor and  communication between each of these  modules  not only does this allow for everything  to be controlled more centrally  for example this system to this system  but also for more proper data collection  and feedback  here is a video of a reel to reel  120  feeding  nitrocellulose membrane  as shown here  being driven  through a system  of biojets dispensing  five antibody lines  these tandem pumps ensure  that the process has never stopped  now this is nitrocellulose for  conjugate  a separate process would be used on the  same system where fiberglass would be  fed to that air job  here you see five lines on  nitrocellulose  being analyzed  with inline vision  and a bad part marker before being dried  and rolled up in the takeout module  following inline region deposition the  layers are assembled into a test and a  process called lamination  auto lamination  includes web speeds up to 100  millimeters per second which is highly  dependent upon what material being run  as well as the number of laminates or  how complicated the test is  this process is designed for quick  alignment and changeover that way  multiple products can be run on a single  system  the system is modular it's flexible it  can be run with multiple applications  and these modular units can be added and  subtracted from the system later as  needed  typically  after lamination  the finished layers are fed to a cutter  for cutting  these can be first fed to master cards  in a reel  to be transferred  or cut in line  here's an example of an auto laminator  from left to right  a module  laying backing card down  before being laminated with  nitrocellulose containing anybody  testing control lines  then having a conjugate pad added  these layers can then be analyzed with  vision for overlap and precision  before being laid with cover tape or  barcode  in this scenario  either the tests are cut in a guillotine  cutter or rolled up for a downstream  process  here is a video of an auto laminator  the backing  material with the take-up liner  the membrane payout with tracking and  take-up liner  and three material layout in this case  including conjugate  the vision inspection  the cover tape payout  and placement  before the cutter  cap stand for tracking  and driving and the take-up reel  in addition to basic lamination  barcode labels can be added  for the test  and provision inspection recognition  after lamination cards can be collected  and stored instead of cutting  or the final assembled test can be  rolled up  [Music]  the last process prior to  bottling or packaging is cutting  these are a couple of the cutting  options besides the guillotine cutter  in this scenario a rolled up test can be  fed  to a cutter  to create specific lengths these cutters  can be  used  with various sensors including a target  sensor for fiducials  in this picture  a bottle collector is added to the end  of the cutter  to collect  the cut test strips  here  shown is a cutter that is used in batch  level manufacturing  pre-cut cards that are laminated  are fed to a cutter  and then bottled  in this picture  a rolled up laminated test  is being fed to a cutter  which then sorts  into two different bottles  besides the standard  lateral flow test that contain testing  control lines  it is possible to create multiplexed  arrays  and geometric symbols  besides multiplexing  this can  lead to more sensitive quantitative  tests but also qualitative tests  to  eliminate the possibility for any  ambiguity or create a certain message  multiplexing can be done at the batch  level or inline web handling  in a high throughput manner  oftentimes this is continuous however  for certain complicated arrays indexing  is important  at a high speed  that  enables multiple arrays per second to be  created  region handling is critical  in particular  oftentimes higher concentrations or more  viscous samples are required  typically  this process is going to be using  picoliters to nanoliters  as opposed to microliters  as shown here  in 50 nanoliter spots on nitrocellulose  positional accuracy is very critical for  this particularly  for multiplex arrays or require some  type of reader  for this reason fiducials can be used  often with a vision system  and software is used for  editing an array which is particularly  ideal  when looking at r d level manufacturing  here is an example of batch level  multiplex array  which include pre-cut card  various dispense channels dispensing an  array  created  your revision program  this process can also be adapted to  in-line manufacturing  often times with the ring for example  here is a schematic or a concept  of material being fed into two separate  dispense modules for indexing to create  these  more complex arrays  before being dried  this concept also shows in line  lamination  and cutting and bottling  which would be the ideal process  oftentimes  customers choose to separate these out  that way  multiple  lots  in addition to multiple tests  can be developed  when looking at multiplexed arrays  particularly in smaller volumes  qc is  especially important for inline  manufacturing  because it's critical to know exactly  when to make a cut and when to dispense  arrays  for this reason fiducial markings are  also used  these  fiducial markings  not only allow  a cutter to trigger cuts  as needed down the process as well as  lamination  but can also be used to trigger  dispensers  to  deposit a very specific pattern  within a certain window  thank you for your time  and thank you dr forensic  for that outstanding presentation  we will now move into the live q a  portion of the presentation and as a  reminder please submit your questions uh  in the q and a box  okay  our first question is  um can you give some specific examples  of arrayed lfds  um  so array lfds are not nearly as common  as the standard lfds stripes with single  lines however typically we'll see  multiplex  tests that  require a higher level of sensitivity  that can be matched up with some sort of  a reader  and one of the big advantages of the  array  is that the customer  can use less reagent and also save costs  on the test uh in particular i have  worked with a couple of these type of  irate lfds in the past that deal with  the detection of certain toxins such as  anthrax  wow okay thank you for that  and the next question that we have is  what have what has been the biggest  challenges when manufacturing cobia 19  tests  so the nice thing about our dispensing  technology  is that  going from different types of tests  different types of biochemistries is  often directly translatable since we can  kind of tune for different fluid uh  realities and properties  in all reality the biggest  challenge that we faced is customers who  are pretty new to this arena to lfd type  of tests jumping into the covet arena  with  some of them little experience  uh the other part of that will be a lot  of customers who  have been more in r d  type of manufacturing jumping to inline  for cova testing so it's mainly been  assisting customers kind of making that  jump in a very short period of time  which has been very common in the last  eight months as you can imagine  thank you so much dr funzak and i have a  few more questions here uh  how many tests per year are possible  when batch manufacturing  uh typically and this is really heavily  dependent upon the actual process  we have seen  a single system manufacturing in the low  to mid hundreds of thousands of tests  per year  um in order to really get closer to the  million tests per year it usually  requires  uh maybe three ships a day and often  multiple pieces of equipment and at some  point it usually makes more sense to  just scale to to the inline  type of equipment for that  so usually it's gonna be in the in the  low to mid hundreds of thousands maybe  up to about 500 000  thank you so much and  our fourth question today is what is the  most challenging lfa application  bio dot has experienced  so typically it's going to be  one of two scenarios  number one  it's often pretty common to select  certain materials particularly  very thin on back nitrocellulose which  is often good  for higher sensitive type of tests  transitioning from batch to inline where  web handling is critical  using those type of materials with  tensile strength is is challenging  uh the other challenge is  and really kind of what i mentioned the  talk of the the time limiting step is  drawing particularly when when  depositing a high volume of reagent  so often a challenge is  to  find a scenario where we can keep a very  high throughput but also making sure  materials are tracked properly materials  do not break in line  and materials are dried before they are  assembled in a single test  thank you so much and our last two  questions there here is what is  typically the biggest challenge when  transitioning from  batch to inline manufacturing  uh  pretty much  the  the answer i just gave uh it  it's mainly drawing um but but really  developing the process around  continuously uh processing  tests for you know an hour or so as  opposed to doing batches it's really  adapting a process and making sure that  that when developing a batch process  it's  easily translatable to in line without  having to go and reinvent the  reinvent the wheel  wonderful  okay and our final question today uh is  how common doctor forensic is it to  combine dispensing laminating and  cutting into a single inline process  the answer is it's not very common  usually lamination and cutting  are combined  the problem again usually is drawing  sufficiently  particularly when running at a higher  speed for good throughput  uh the other thing is is that a lot of  times customers are running multiple  types of tests so it doesn't make sense  to have everything in line that way they  can easily switch over to what has to  dispense  and then at the same time they can be  laminating and cutting for another type  of test if that kind of makes sense so  usually it's not very efficient plus  um an inline system that combines all  three would be very long  so it work it would require  quite a bit of space usually instead of  having a nice you know long  process like that it usually makes sense  to stack systems side by side for space  purposes  thank you again dr franzak and  unfortunately we are out of time for  today  if you have any questions that were  submitted and not answered today uh by  our speaker we'll be addressed via email  and  thank you for your outstanding questions  to the audience and the presentation  will be available on demand for viewing  in for 12 months please remember to  share it with colleagues who might be  interested in today's topic  and don't miss out on other  presentations on our agenda visit the  agenda tab in the auditorium for a full  listing thank you again for your  participation and until next time  thank you
 

